        "0": {
          "id": "0",
          "name": "main.py",
          "language": "py",
          "contents": "# CIW AI Associate Standard: 2.1\n# Standard Description: Describe a simple model of the data processing cycle (input-processing-output).\n#\n# Rationale: Building a model involves many steps: cleaning, scaling, and training. \n# We call this a \"Pipeline\". A Pipeline ensures that every transformation applied \n# to the training data is ALSO applied to the test data automatically, reducing errors.\n\n\"\"\"\nWelcome! A true AI engineer doesn't just run one model. They build a \"Pipeline\".\nThink of an assembly line in a factory:\n1. Raw materials enter (Data).\n2. Machines process it (Scaling, Cleaning).\n3. The final product is made (Prediction).\n\nWe use scikit-learn's 'Pipeline' to automate this!\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_breast_cancer\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nimport numpy as np\n\n# --- LOAD DATA ---\n# Let's introduce some missing values to make it harder!\ndata = load_breast_cancer()\nX, y = data.data, data.target\n\n# Randomly replace 20 values with NaN (missing)\nrng = np.random.RandomState(42)\nmask = rng.rand(*X.shape) < 0.1 # 10% missing data\nX[mask] = np.nan\n\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, random_state=42)\n\n# --- PART 1: THE PIPELINE ---\nprint(\"--- Part 1: Building the Pipeline ---\")\n# Our pipeline will have 3 steps:\n# 1. Imputer: Fill in missing values.\n# 2. Scaler: Scale the features.\n# 3. Classifier: Make the prediction.\n\npipeline = Pipeline([\n    ('imputer', SimpleImputer(strategy='mean')),\n    ('scaler', StandardScaler()),\n    ('classifier', LogisticRegression())\n])\n\n# We treat the pipeline just like a regular model!\n# We call .fit(), and it automatically runs Step 1 -> Step 2 -> Step 3.\npipeline.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\n# When we predict, it also runs Step 1 -> Step 2 -> Step 3 on the test data!\nscore = pipeline.score(X_test, y_test)\n\nprint(f\"Pipeline Accuracy: {score:.2%}\")\nprint(\"\\nNotice: We didn't have to manually transform X_test!\")\nprint(\"The pipeline remembered exactly how to scale and impute based on X_train.\")\n\n\"\"\"\n--- YOUR TURN ---\nWe've set up a regression problem (predicting numbers) with missing data.\n1. Create a Pipeline with TWO steps:\n   - 'imputer': SimpleImputer(strategy='median')\n   - 'regressor': LinearRegression()\n2. Fit the pipeline to X_train_reg, y_train_reg.\n3. Print the R^2 score on the test set.\n\"\"\"\n\n# STARTING CODE FOR STUDENT:\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_diabetes\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\ndiabetes = load_diabetes()\nX_reg, y_reg = diabetes.data, diabetes.target\n\n# Add some missing values\nrng = np.random.RandomState(42)\nmask = rng.rand(*X_reg.shape) < 0.1\nX_reg[mask] = np.nan\n\nX_train_reg, X_test_reg, y_train_reg, y_test_reg = train_test_split(X_reg, y_reg, random_state=42)\n\n# TODO: Create the pipeline\n# my_pipeline = Pipeline([ ... ])\n\n# TODO: Fit the pipeline\n\n# TODO: Score the pipeline!\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": true
        }
